WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.008 --> 00:00:12.011
Hi, my name is Rama Ranganathan, I'm a Professor at the Green Center for Systems Biology

00:00:12.012 --> 00:00:15.014
and affiliated with the departments of physics and pharmacology

00:00:15.015 --> 00:00:17.016
at UT Southwestern Medical Center

00:00:17.017 --> 00:00:21.020
And I'll be doing a talk in this series

00:00:21.021 --> 00:00:25.024
called "great questions in life sciences, intersection between biology, computation, and physics

00:00:25.025 --> 00:00:28.027
Specifically, the problem I would like to ask

00:00:28.028 --> 00:00:33.032
is a very interesting one, it's about finding the effective variables

00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:39.038
in biological systems. So what I like to do is to describe the nature of this problem, to tell you why

00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:44.043
finding these effective variables is an important yet a difficult problem

00:00:44.044 --> 00:00:49.048
I'll try to  propose an approach that leads to specific problems of interest

00:00:49.049 --> 00:00:54.053
people coming from quantitative sciences into biology. So a grand goal in biology

00:00:54.054 --> 00:00:59.058
is to quantitatively explain the behavior and evolutionary origin of so called

00:00:59.059 --> 00:01:05.064
complex systems at many scale. I'm showing biological systems at a variety of scales

00:01:05.065 --> 00:01:10.069
from the atomic scale, that is the structure and function of proteins

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:15.074
to things at the cellular scale, the designate of cellular macromolecules and systems

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:21.080
like the flagellum, to the design of cells and tissues

00:01:21.081 --> 00:01:24.083
and ultimately the design and principles of ecosystems

00:01:24.084 --> 00:01:29.088
the goal is to explain the behavior and the origin at all these scales

00:01:29.089 --> 00:01:32.091
but the problem is difficult for 3 reasons. The first is

00:01:32.092 --> 00:01:39.098
the problem of heterogeneity. In all these scales, system components, the things that make the parts of

00:01:39.099 --> 00:01:43.102
make up these systems are highly heterogeneous where some of the parts and connections

00:01:43.103 --> 00:01:45.104
are much more important than others.

00:01:45.105 --> 00:01:49.108
For example, we know that in proteins, not all of the amino acids act equally

00:01:49.109 --> 00:01:52.111
In fact, there a heterogeneity in the importance of amino acids

00:01:52.112 --> 00:01:58.117
towards the central properties of proteins such as foldability, biochemical functions, and so on

00:01:58.118 --> 00:02:04.123
the same thing is true at a subcellular systems and a macromolecular systems

00:02:04.124 --> 00:02:09.128
the importance of the parts is heterogeneous and often is not obvious

00:02:09.129 --> 00:02:13.132
when looking at those systems which are the parts that are important, which are the parts that are less important

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:15.134
the second problem is that of cooperativity

00:02:15.135 --> 00:02:21.140
that is the systems are made up of parts, but the parts work in a way that cannot predict the whole

00:02:21.141 --> 00:02:25.144
cannot be predicted in the activities of the parts when calculated individually

00:02:25.145 --> 00:02:31.150
there is also potentially nonlinearity in the system

00:02:31.151 --> 00:02:39.158
so that the behavior of the system taken overall is not an obviously decomposition into the activity of the parts taken individually

00:02:39.159 --> 00:02:47.166
the third problem is the problem of modularity in that different groups of a component can be grouped in a quasi-independent way

00:02:47.167 --> 00:02:52.171
to provide different aspect of function at all these scales

00:02:52.172 --> 00:02:57.176
So the existence of the properties, the facts that these parts that make up these systems

00:02:57.177 --> 00:03:03.182
the fact that these amino acids make up proteins, proteins and small molecules that make up macromolecular complexes and cells

00:03:03.183 --> 00:03:08.187
cells that make up tissues, and species that make up ecosystems

00:03:08.188 --> 00:03:18.197
the fact that these component descriptions involves heterogeneous, cooperative groups imply that what we need is a

00:03:18.198 --> 00:03:21.200
fundamental re-parameterization of these systems to redo the

00:03:21.201 --> 00:03:25.204
fine units of function or what I call the effective variables

00:03:25.205 --> 00:03:32.211
as not these parts, but groups of these parts that act cooperatively, and ideally ranked by their relevance

00:03:32.212 --> 00:03:35.214
the question is how can we do that?

00:03:35.215 --> 00:03:45.224
The goal in doing this is to provide some sense of what natural description of what a system is. It may be intuitive

00:03:45.225 --> 00:03:51.230
think about a protein as made up of amino acids, but in terms of the function of the protein,

00:03:51.231 --> 00:03:57.236
It may not be the actual description. Groups of amino acids work cooperatively may be a more natural description

00:03:57.237 --> 00:04:05.244
of what the system is. And in some sense, one could argue that solving this problem, providing a more natural description of what a system is

00:04:05.245 --> 00:04:09.248
should be a necessary preclude for mechanistic studies of how it works

00:04:09.249 --> 00:04:17.256
another words, understanding how it works may be simpler if we understand the effective variables that make up a system

00:04:17.257 --> 00:04:20.259
The question is how can we do this practically?

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:25.264
Well it turns out with the massive expansion of techniques for massive data collection

00:04:25.265 --> 00:04:32.271
for all these scales, people are collecting methods for collecting many examples of protein structures

00:04:32.272 --> 00:04:36.275
and protein sequences and measurements of protein function

00:04:36.276 --> 00:04:43.282
of course, many are working at the cellular scale to characterize many different cell types under the background of perturbation for example mutations

00:04:43.283 --> 00:04:47.286
and small molecule perturbations

00:04:47.287 --> 00:04:51.290
and even at the ecosystem scale,  more recently people have developed synthetic ecosystem laboratories

00:04:51.291 --> 00:04:59.298
with many replicates so people can look at the system's behavior at that scale

00:04:59.299 --> 00:05:08.307
so with the ability to collect data in nature. The proposal is to look not at individual systems

00:05:08.308 --> 00:05:16.315
but at statistics and many realizations of this system to see what is conserved or invariant over the ensemble

00:05:16.316 --> 00:05:23.322
fundamentally, the variants of properties of an ensemble of realizations of a system

00:05:23.323 --> 00:05:27.326
is itself a relevance in systems components and reactions

00:05:27.327 --> 00:05:31.330
things that are invariant over an ensemble tends to be relevant

00:05:31.331 --> 00:05:34.333
the second thing to do since we are interested in the cooperativity of components

00:05:34.334 --> 00:05:42.341
is to look at correlations and epistasis between the conserved system components and reactions

00:05:42.342 --> 00:05:50.349
and this is a basis, such a correlational analysis is a basis for decomposing into the proper cooperative modular units

00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:58.357
that is the effective variables. Let me give an example of this, at the scale of atoms that we've been talking about.

00:05:58.358 --> 00:06:03.362
but the approach should be more general than just this case study

00:06:03.363 --> 00:06:09.368
So here is an example where the protein can be thought of as made up of a large collection of amino acids

00:06:09.369 --> 00:06:15.374
that interact together, but to understand which amino acids are relevant and what are the cooperative units

00:06:15.375 --> 00:06:19.378
this kind of approach, one collects an appropriate statistical ensemble

00:06:19.379 --> 00:06:29.388
of instances of a protein, in this case, a protein family that shares the property of folding into that structure and carrying out the biochemical activities

00:06:29.389 --> 00:06:39.398
and from such a statistical ensemble, one can make a matrix of relevant correlations of all the  amino acids that make up the protein

00:06:39.399 --> 00:06:43.402
and with that correlational matrix at hand

00:06:43.403 --> 00:06:47.406
one can apply techniques such as structural decomposition

00:06:47.407 --> 00:06:51.410
very simple way of information content in correlation matrices

00:06:51.411 --> 00:06:58.417
to deduce collective groups of amino acids that now more appropriately represent the determinants

00:06:58.418 --> 00:07:02.421
of protein structure and function. So this is an example at the atomic scale

00:07:02.422 --> 00:07:09.428
but I want to point out that there are non-trivial quantitative problems at every stage of analysis

00:07:09.429 --> 00:07:14.433
in devising this point of approach more generally for all biological complex systems

00:07:14.434 --> 00:07:18.437
and let me just illustrate the nature of the problems that need to be worked on

00:07:18.438 --> 00:07:24.443
For example it's not obvious when you take a statistical approach how to make an ensemble that represents

00:07:24.444 --> 00:07:30.449
the properties of interest. In case of proteins, it seems nature that a protein family

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:36.455
that shares the function of folding and function and an evolutionary history

00:07:36.456 --> 00:07:40.459
should be an appropriate ensemble over which the statistics is computed

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:47.466
but it's not necessarily obvious in many other systems, at a cellular scale, at the scale of ecosystems and so on

00:07:47.467 --> 00:07:52.471
what should be an appropriate ensemble of data to collect

00:07:52.472 --> 00:07:57.476
from which statistics can reveal the underlying variables of a system

00:07:57.477 --> 00:08:03.482
that's a working problem to solve. The second problem is what type of statistical system one should apply

00:08:03.483 --> 00:08:11.490
to make measures of relevance and correlation given an ensemble and the reason for this is

00:08:11.491 --> 00:08:19.498
it depends on how the ensemble is collected for protein sequences. one of the complications for making appropriate statistics is that

00:08:19.499 --> 00:08:24.504
the sequences are not randomly selected from the space of all sequences

00:08:24.505 --> 00:08:31.511
these sequeces have a relationship to each other that comes from the phylogeny of all the species that contain that sequences

00:08:31.512 --> 00:08:39.519
in other words, sampling is not random, but sampling is on some phylogenetic tree, and in such instances

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:50.530
it not necessary obvious what are appropriate statistical methods that define the relevance of the outcomes at positions or correlations between positions

00:08:50.531 --> 00:08:57.537
So in general, one needs to work on what are the statistical methods that are appropriate to represent a statistical ensemble

00:08:57.538 --> 00:09:05.545
the final problem is, how to decompose these correlational matrices to deduce the effective variables

00:09:05.546 --> 00:09:10.550
in the simplest case, one apply linear methods such as spectral decomposition

00:09:10.551 --> 00:09:20.560
but it's not so obvious that those decompositions are good decompositions of this information. In general, information in these correlational

00:09:20.561 --> 00:09:26.566
in biological systems is hierarchical in nature. And for a hierarchically embedded system,

00:09:26.567 --> 00:09:32.572
it's not yet clear what are good methods are in finding the effective variables

00:09:32.573 --> 00:09:39.579
so these are problems are ripe to work on now for people coming into biology from quantitative sciences

00:09:39.580 --> 00:09:50.590
because if these sort of questions can be addressed in general, then we have the possibility leading to really new understandings

00:09:50.591 --> 00:09:54.594
of the so called design principles of complex biological systems

00:09:54.595 --> 00:09:59.599
to understand the effective variables underlying these systems, as I said a preload

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:04.604
to ultimately deep mechanistic studies of these systems

00:10:04.605 --> 00:10:13.613
so this is an example of a problem that is appropriate and ripe today for people in physics and quantitative sciences

00:10:13.614 --> 00:10:42.309
coming into biology.  Thank you very much!

